2025-05-01 03:17:46 - Thread-1273 (execute) - INFO - [Exploration]
Relevant columns and potential usage  
• payment.customer_id: links each payment to a customer  
• payment.amount: the money paid in that transaction – needed for any “change in payment amounts” calculation  
• payment.payment_date: contains the exact date‑time, from which we will derive “YYYY‑MM” to identify a month  
• customer.first_name / customer.last_name: to display the requested full customer name  
Conditions / joins that will be used  
• JOIN customer       ON customer.customer_id = payment.customer_id  
• Generate a “year‑month” key with strftime('%Y‑%m', payment_date)  
• Aggregate SUM(amount) per customer per month  
• Use LAG(...) OVER (PARTITION BY customer_id ORDER BY year‑month) to find the difference between consecutive months  
• Average those monthly differences (absolute value) for every customer  
• Pick the customer with the highest average difference  

Below are ten exploratory‑to‑final SQLite queries (all SELECT only).  
Each block is preceded by a short description and every query is limited to 20 rows.

```sql
-- 1. Peek at a handful of customers
SELECT DISTINCT customer_id,
       first_name || ' ' || last_name AS full_name
FROM   customer
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 2. Peek at raw payment rows
SELECT DISTINCT payment_id,
       customer_id,
       amount,
       payment_date
FROM   payment
ORDER  BY payment_id
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 3. Which customers actually have payments recorded?
SELECT DISTINCT customer_id
FROM   payment
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 4. What calendar months appear in the payments table?
SELECT DISTINCT strftime('%Y-%m', payment_date) AS year_month
FROM   payment
ORDER  BY year_month
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 5. Monthly payment totals for each customer (first 20 rows)
SELECT *
FROM (
    SELECT customer_id,
           strftime('%Y-%m', payment_date) AS year_month,
           SUM(amount)                     AS monthly_amount
    FROM   payment
    GROUP  BY customer_id, year_month
    ORDER  BY customer_id, year_month
) 
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 6. Add month‑to‑month difference using LAG (first 20 rows)
SELECT *
FROM (
    SELECT customer_id,
           year_month,
           monthly_amount,
           monthly_amount 
             - LAG(monthly_amount) OVER (PARTITION BY customer_id
                                         ORDER BY year_month) AS monthly_diff
    FROM (
        SELECT customer_id,
               strftime('%Y-%m', payment_date) AS year_month,
               SUM(amount)                     AS monthly_amount
        FROM   payment
        GROUP  BY customer_id, year_month
    )
)
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 7. Average absolute monthly change per customer
SELECT customer_id,
       AVG(ABS(monthly_diff)) AS avg_monthly_change
FROM (
    SELECT customer_id,
           year_month,
           monthly_amount,
           monthly_amount 
             - LAG(monthly_amount) OVER (PARTITION BY customer_id
                                         ORDER BY year_month) AS monthly_diff
    FROM (
        SELECT customer_id,
               strftime('%Y-%m', payment_date) AS year_month,
               SUM(amount)                     AS monthly_amount
        FROM   payment
        GROUP  BY customer_id, year_month
    )
)
WHERE  monthly_diff IS NOT NULL
GROUP BY customer_id
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 8. Who are the top 20 customers by average monthly change?
SELECT *
FROM (
    SELECT customer_id,
           AVG(ABS(monthly_diff)) AS avg_monthly_change
    FROM (
        SELECT customer_id,
               year_month,
               monthly_amount,
               monthly_amount 
                 - LAG(monthly_amount) OVER (PARTITION BY customer_id
                                             ORDER BY year_month) AS monthly_diff
        FROM (
            SELECT customer_id,
                   strftime('%Y-%m', payment_date) AS year_month,
                   SUM(amount)                     AS monthly_amount
            FROM   payment
            GROUP  BY customer_id, year_month
        )
    )
    WHERE  monthly_diff IS NOT NULL
    GROUP BY customer_id
    ORDER BY avg_monthly_change DESC
)
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 9. Grab only the single customer_id with the highest average change
SELECT customer_id
FROM (
    SELECT customer_id,
           AVG(ABS(monthly_diff)) AS avg_monthly_change
    FROM (
        SELECT customer_id,
               year_month,
               monthly_amount,
               monthly_amount 
                 - LAG(monthly_amount) OVER (PARTITION BY customer_id
                                             ORDER BY year_month) AS monthly_diff
        FROM (
            SELECT customer_id,
                   strftime('%Y-%m', payment_date) AS year_month,
                   SUM(amount)                     AS monthly_amount
            FROM   payment
            GROUP  BY customer_id, year_month
        )
    )
    WHERE  monthly_diff IS NOT NULL
    GROUP BY customer_id
    ORDER BY avg_monthly_change DESC
    LIMIT 1
);
```

```sql
-- 10. Final answer: full name of the customer with the highest average monthly change
SELECT c.first_name || ' ' || c.last_name AS full_name
FROM   customer AS c
JOIN  (
    SELECT customer_id
    FROM (
        SELECT customer_id,
               AVG(ABS(monthly_diff)) AS avg_monthly_change
        FROM (
            SELECT customer_id,
                   year_month,
                   monthly_amount,
                   monthly_amount 
                     - LAG(monthly_amount) OVER (PARTITION BY customer_id
                                                 ORDER BY year_month) AS monthly_diff
            FROM (
                SELECT customer_id,
                       strftime('%Y-%m', payment_date) AS year_month,
                       SUM(amount)                     AS monthly_amount
                FROM   payment
                GROUP  BY customer_id, year_month
            )
        )
        WHERE  monthly_diff IS NOT NULL
        GROUP BY customer_id
        ORDER BY avg_monthly_change DESC
        LIMIT 1
    )
) AS top ON c.customer_id = top.customer_id
LIMIT 20;
```
